1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,734
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,754 --> 00:00:08,660
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:16,080
This is Lecture 18, designing, building and testing the control box.

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:20,557
So far we've assumed the control box just works.

5
00:00:20,577 --> 00:00:26,532
In reality it sits under the table, thirty or forty degrees in summer,

6
00:00:26,552 --> 00:00:28,324
lint floating around,

7
00:00:28,344 --> 00:00:33,446
the main shaft starting and stopping a thousand times a day,

8
00:00:33,466 --> 00:00:35,921
and it has to last ten years.

9
00:00:36,321 --> 00:00:40,882
Three threads today, heat, interference and burn-in.

10
00:00:40,902 --> 00:00:44,406
Requirements and standards, the V-model,

11
00:00:44,426 --> 00:00:51,541
software layers and the production test flow are left to the slides and the quiz.

12
00:00:53,061 --> 00:00:54,032
Here's the problem.

13
00:00:54,432 --> 00:01:01,666
A model of servo control box for lockstitch machines has been in service for

14
00:01:01,686 --> 00:01:02,812
a few years.

15
00:01:03,212 --> 00:01:05,321
Spring and autumn are fine,

16
00:01:05,341 --> 00:01:11,234
but every summer several factories report frequent overheating fault codes;

17
00:01:11,254 --> 00:01:14,152
after a rest the machine works again.

18
00:01:14,552 --> 00:01:20,132
Some returned units also have bulging DC-link capacitors.

19
00:01:20,532 --> 00:01:25,541
These floors have no air conditioning and reach about 38 degrees on summer

20
00:01:25,561 --> 00:01:26,288
afternoons.

21
00:01:26,688 --> 00:01:35,708
The heat sink alarms at 100 degrees, and the switches' junction limit is 125.

22
00:01:36,108 --> 00:01:41,208
Is the design inadequate, or is something else going on?

23
00:01:41,608 --> 00:01:42,827
We'll answer at the end.

24
00:01:44,347 --> 00:01:47,588
Heat: losses and thermal resistance.

25
00:01:49,108 --> 00:01:50,808
Where does the heat come from?

26
00:01:51,208 --> 00:01:56,686
Each switch in the inverter bridge has two kinds of loss.

27
00:01:57,086 --> 00:02:02,408
Conduction loss: roughly a 1-volt drop plus a resistive drop,

28
00:02:02,428 --> 00:02:05,824
so one term grows with current squared.

29
00:02:06,224 --> 00:02:10,116
Switching loss: while a switch turns on or off,

30
00:02:10,136 --> 00:02:14,360
voltage and current are both non-zero for a moment,

31
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:20,437
and the average power is about half the DC-link voltage times the average

32
00:02:20,457 --> 00:02:25,764
current times the transition time times the switching frequency.

33
00:02:26,164 --> 00:02:35,399
In the example the whole power stage dissipates about 20 watts at 3 amps and

34
00:02:35,419 --> 00:02:37,103
about 74 at 9.

35
00:02:37,504 --> 00:02:42,331
Three times the current, nearly four times the loss.

36
00:02:43,851 --> 00:02:47,024
Heat leaves the switch's chip, the junction,

37
00:02:47,044 --> 00:02:51,815
passes through the package and thermal interface to the heat sink,

38
00:02:51,835 --> 00:02:54,210
then into the air inside the box,

39
00:02:54,230 --> 00:02:57,911
and finally out through the case into the workshop.

40
00:02:58,311 --> 00:03:03,731
Each stage is a thermal resistance in kelvin per watt.

41
00:03:04,131 --> 00:03:10,195
Treat it like a circuit: temperature is voltage, heat is current,

42
00:03:10,215 --> 00:03:17,777
and the junction temperature is the ambient plus the rise across each resistance.

43
00:03:18,177 --> 00:03:25,290
In the example: 3.5 from junction to sink, 1.5 from sink to internal air,

44
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:31,739
and the internal air sits 0.6 times the total heat above the room.

45
00:03:32,139 --> 00:03:37,447
Note that the heat sink carries the whole power stage's heat,

46
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:42,599
while the junction-to-sink stage carries only one switch's.

47
00:03:44,119 --> 00:03:50,259
Here are current and temperature over two sewing cycles from the lab.

48
00:03:50,659 --> 00:03:52,710
Grey is phase current,

49
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:58,641
highest during the fraction of a second of each start and stop;

50
00:03:58,661 --> 00:04:03,159
red is junction temperature, blue the heat sink.

51
00:04:03,559 --> 00:04:09,532
The heat sink has a large thermal mass and a time constant of minutes,

52
00:04:09,552 --> 00:04:14,926
so its temperature follows the average loss and is almost flat.

53
00:04:15,326 --> 00:04:16,672
The chip is tiny,

54
00:04:16,692 --> 00:04:22,134
with a junction-to-sink time constant of only about 50 milliseconds,

55
00:04:22,154 --> 00:04:25,106
so the junction jumps at every start.

56
00:04:25,506 --> 00:04:30,105
Watch the starts per minute go from 15 to 60:

57
00:04:30,125 --> 00:04:35,340
the blue line rises and the red peaks rise with it.

58
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:43,328
Run at 9 amps continuously and the heat sink would reach 200 degrees:

59
00:04:43,348 --> 00:04:44,362
impossible.

60
00:04:44,762 --> 00:04:48,582
It works only because sewing is intermittent.

61
00:04:48,982 --> 00:04:52,843
With 30 starts a minute and sewing half the time,

62
00:04:52,863 --> 00:04:57,673
the average loss is about 18 watts, and the starts and stops,

63
00:04:57,693 --> 00:05:03,137
only 15 percent of the time, contribute about 62 percent of the heat.

64
00:05:03,537 --> 00:05:10,842
In a 40-degree room the heat sink is about 83 degrees and the junction peaks

65
00:05:10,862 --> 00:05:12,095
at about 110.

66
00:05:12,495 --> 00:05:18,158
Thermal design has two layers: average loss sets the sink,

67
00:05:18,178 --> 00:05:23,450
peak current sets how far the junction jumps above it.

68
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:30,142
Interference: common-mode current and switching speed.

69
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:34,722
Now electromagnetic compatibility.

70
00:05:35,122 --> 00:05:37,884
The box is itself a noise source:

71
00:05:37,904 --> 00:05:42,773
the inverter switches tens of thousands of times a second,

72
00:05:42,793 --> 00:05:49,010
each time swinging from 0 to 310 volts in tens to hundreds of nanoseconds.

73
00:05:49,410 --> 00:05:56,902
It must also withstand others' noise: sags and surges, fast transient bursts,

74
00:05:56,922 --> 00:05:58,853
kilovolts of static.

75
00:05:59,253 --> 00:06:02,493
The key idea is common-mode current.

76
00:06:02,893 --> 00:06:09,991
There's parasitic capacitance between the motor winding and the earthed frame,

77
00:06:10,011 --> 00:06:15,923
so every voltage step pushes a current pulse through it to earth,

78
00:06:15,943 --> 00:06:21,307
which then loops back through the earth wire and the mains.

79
00:06:21,707 --> 00:06:34,268
With 300 picofarads and 310 volts in 150 nanoseconds, that's about 0.6 amps.

80
00:06:35,788 --> 00:06:38,888
Now connect heat and interference.

81
00:06:39,288 --> 00:06:45,892
The horizontal axis is switching time; red is peak junction temperature,

82
00:06:45,912 --> 00:06:48,468
blue is common-mode current.

83
00:06:48,868 --> 00:06:55,089
A longer switching time means lower dv/dt and less common-mode current,

84
00:06:55,109 --> 00:06:59,747
but switching loss is proportional to switching time,

85
00:06:59,767 --> 00:07:02,208
so the junction gets hotter.

86
00:07:02,608 --> 00:07:08,001
Watch the green dot move from 100 nanoseconds to 300 to 600:

87
00:07:08,021 --> 00:07:14,406
at one end the noise is too high, at the other the junction is too hot,

88
00:07:14,426 --> 00:07:19,548
and only the green window in between meets both criteria.

89
00:07:21,068 --> 00:07:24,317
Software, production and burn-in.

90
00:07:25,837 --> 00:07:26,617
Why burn in?

91
00:07:27,017 --> 00:07:30,727
Failure rate over time looks like a bathtub:

92
00:07:30,747 --> 00:07:36,152
early failures from bad joints and defective parts at the start,

93
00:07:36,172 --> 00:07:40,983
which factory tests can't always catch; then a long, low,

94
00:07:41,003 --> 00:07:46,662
flat stretch of random failures; and finally wear-out as parts age.

95
00:07:47,062 --> 00:07:52,497
The chart shows the first half of the bathtub on log scales.

96
00:07:52,897 --> 00:07:58,587
Burn-in means the box spends part of its early life at the factory:

97
00:07:58,607 --> 00:08:02,849
watch the burn-in time go from 0 to 8 to 24 hours,

98
00:08:02,869 --> 00:08:05,917
the grey equivalent-use area widens,

99
00:08:05,937 --> 00:08:09,837
and early failures are caught before shipping.

100
00:08:11,357 --> 00:08:17,342
Burn-in at room temperature is too slow, so it's done hot and under load,

101
00:08:17,362 --> 00:08:22,854
with the acceleration factor estimated from the Arrhenius relation.

102
00:08:23,254 --> 00:08:27,433
Note that T_bi is the component temperature, not the oven's.

103
00:08:27,833 --> 00:08:29,307
In the example,

104
00:08:29,327 --> 00:08:36,576
with 0.7 electron-volts activation energy and parts at 55 degrees in use,

105
00:08:36,596 --> 00:08:40,957
a 70-degree oven puts the parts at about 85,

106
00:08:40,977 --> 00:08:45,238
giving an acceleration factor of about 8.0:

107
00:08:45,258 --> 00:08:49,420
8 hours of burn-in equals 64 hours of use.

108
00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:54,639
If 1 percent of boxes have early defects,

109
00:08:54,659 --> 00:09:03,136
first-year returns are about 1.47 percent without burn-in and about 0.92

110
00:09:03,156 --> 00:09:06,440
percent after 8 hours at 70.

111
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:11,119
Case: overheating every summer.

112
00:09:12,639 --> 00:09:14,339
Back to the opening question.

113
00:09:14,739 --> 00:09:20,847
A clean box at 38 degrees has its heat sink at about 81 and its junction

114
00:09:20,867 --> 00:09:22,634
peaking at about 108,

115
00:09:22,654 --> 00:09:28,251
with margin to both the 100-degree alarm and the 125-degree limit,

116
00:09:28,271 --> 00:09:30,719
so the design itself is fine.

117
00:09:31,119 --> 00:09:33,353
Open a returned unit, though,

118
00:09:33,373 --> 00:09:37,939
and the fins and vents are matted with lint like a blanket.

119
00:09:38,339 --> 00:09:43,409
Watch the blockage go from 0 to 0.3 to 0.5:

120
00:09:43,429 --> 00:09:52,524
the blue heat-sink line climbs over the 100-degree alarm and the red junction

121
00:09:52,544 --> 00:09:54,655
peaks go past 125.

122
00:09:56,175 --> 00:10:04,489
At a blockage of 0.5 the heat sink is about 109 degrees, over the alarm,

123
00:10:04,509 --> 00:10:10,508
and the junction peaks at about 136, over the limit.

124
00:10:10,908 --> 00:10:15,888
So the overheating alarm is protection doing exactly its job.

125
00:10:16,288 --> 00:10:21,589
The bulging capacitors have the same cause: by the ten-degree rule,

126
00:10:21,609 --> 00:10:24,290
life halves with every 10 degrees.

127
00:10:24,690 --> 00:10:33,072
Clean, the capacitor runs at about 59 degrees and lasts about 10.4 years;

128
00:10:33,092 --> 00:10:40,092
at 0.5 blockage it runs at about 66 and lasts only about 6.1.

129
00:10:41,612 --> 00:10:46,903
The servo drives of collaborative robots and handling arms are thermally

130
00:10:46,923 --> 00:10:49,116
designed exactly the same way.

131
00:10:49,516 --> 00:10:55,543
In one pick-and-place cycle every joint accelerates, cruises, brakes and stops,

132
00:10:55,563 --> 00:10:59,371
and current peaks during acceleration and braking,

133
00:10:59,391 --> 00:11:02,892
just like a sewing machine's starts and stops.

134
00:11:03,292 --> 00:11:08,400
The drive's heat-sink temperature follows the average loss over a cycle,

135
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:13,456
while the switches' junction temperature follows the peak current while

136
00:11:13,476 --> 00:11:14,382
accelerating,

137
00:11:14,402 --> 00:11:19,581
so you size a drive from the real cycle's average loss and then check the

138
00:11:19,601 --> 00:11:20,579
peak junction.

139
00:11:20,979 --> 00:11:24,882
Many cobots put the drive inside the joint,

140
00:11:24,902 --> 00:11:29,079
where the only heat sink is the joint housing.

141
00:11:30,599 --> 00:11:34,876
The everyday version of this case is a laptop.

142
00:11:35,276 --> 00:11:40,904
After two or three years the fan vents are packed with dust;

143
00:11:40,924 --> 00:11:48,056
under a heavy program the fan roars, the machine gets hot and it slows down.

144
00:11:48,456 --> 00:11:56,514
That's the thermal resistance going up and the processor throttling itself to

145
00:11:56,534 --> 00:11:58,403
avoid overheating,

146
00:11:58,423 --> 00:12:03,124
exactly like a control box derating when hot.

147
00:12:03,524 --> 00:12:08,544
Blow out the dust and both temperature and speed come back.

148
00:12:08,944 --> 00:12:13,561
On the shop floor, lint is the control box's dust.

149
00:12:15,081 --> 00:12:17,780
Answering the opening question:

150
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:24,272
a clean box at 38 degrees has its heat sink at about 81 and doesn't alarm,

151
00:12:24,292 --> 00:12:26,202
so the design is fine.

152
00:12:26,602 --> 00:12:34,829
At a lint blockage of 0.5 the heat sink reaches 109 and alarms,

153
00:12:34,849 --> 00:12:40,981
and the junction and capacitor life suffer too.

154
00:12:41,381 --> 00:12:49,103
Cut the heat-sink resistance from 1.5 to 1.0 and the sink drops to about 93

155
00:12:49,123 --> 00:12:53,128
with the junction peaking at about 120:

156
00:12:53,148 --> 00:12:54,677
no more alarms.

157
00:12:55,077 --> 00:12:58,598
Add regular lint cleaning, thermal derating,

158
00:12:58,618 --> 00:13:04,233
long-life capacitors and conformal coating, and the problem is solved.

159
00:13:05,753 --> 00:13:11,030
Now Virtual lab 18-1, the control box, which you can do yourself on WenQuest.

160
00:13:11,050 --> 00:13:15,019
The criteria on the right and the tasks below are blurred,

161
00:13:15,039 --> 00:13:17,289
so you can read them on your own.

162
00:13:17,309 --> 00:13:21,898
Top left are current and temperature over two sewing cycles; below,

163
00:13:21,918 --> 00:13:23,893
the switching-time trade-off.

164
00:13:24,293 --> 00:13:29,723
First press Summer plus clogging, 38 degrees and a blockage of 0.5,

165
00:13:29,743 --> 00:13:35,499
then raise the blockage further. The blue and red lines climb together.

166
00:13:35,519 --> 00:13:38,428
Which criteria fail? Note them down.

167
00:13:38,828 --> 00:13:39,545
Task one:

168
00:13:39,565 --> 00:13:45,526
leave the room and the duty cycle alone and change only design parameters

169
00:13:45,546 --> 00:13:47,902
until every criterion passes.

170
00:13:47,922 --> 00:13:54,293
Try lowering the heat-sink resistance. The 1.0 on the answer slide is one fix;

171
00:13:54,313 --> 00:13:59,127
which other design changes also work, and which helps most,

172
00:13:59,147 --> 00:14:00,765
is yours to compare.

173
00:14:01,165 --> 00:14:05,747
Reset to standard, set the room to 45 degrees,

174
00:14:05,767 --> 00:14:11,650
and stretch the switching time from 100 nanoseconds to 700.

175
00:14:11,670 --> 00:14:18,953
Watch the dot on the trade-off chart: noisy at one end, hot at the other.

176
00:14:19,353 --> 00:14:20,136
Task two:

177
00:14:20,156 --> 00:14:27,181
raise the switching frequency to 20 kilohertz and re-select the switching time.

178
00:14:27,201 --> 00:14:33,155
Measure how wide the green window is, compare it with 12 kilohertz,

179
00:14:33,175 --> 00:14:36,544
and say which criterion sets each end.

180
00:14:36,944 --> 00:14:40,746
Then page two, burn-in and returns. At 55 degrees,

181
00:14:40,766 --> 00:14:46,708
take the burn-in time from 0 to 24 hours, and the grey equivalent time widens.

182
00:14:46,728 --> 00:14:47,548
Task three:

183
00:14:47,568 --> 00:14:53,358
choose a temperature and a time that keep first-year returns under 1 percent

184
00:14:53,378 --> 00:14:55,803
at no more than 9 yuan per unit.

185
00:14:56,203 --> 00:15:02,029
Try raising the chamber temperature; the cheapest combination is yours to find.

