214
00:17:14,593 --> 00:17:20,951
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example, with a 310-volt DC link,

215
00:17:20,971 --> 00:17:27,779
12 kilohertz, an average current of 3 amps and 300 nanoseconds of switching,

216
00:17:27,799 --> 00:17:31,013
what is one switch's switching loss?

217
00:17:31,413 --> 00:17:37,720
Or, if the main program runs away, what guarantees that the motor stops?

218
00:17:37,740 --> 00:17:44,399
Others ask what in-circuit test actually measures and how burn-in conditions

219
00:17:44,419 --> 00:17:45,366
are chosen.

220
00:17:45,766 --> 00:17:51,386
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

221
00:17:52,906 --> 00:17:54,606
Three things to remember.

222
00:17:55,006 --> 00:18:01,241
One: a control box has to last ten years through voltage swings, heat, lint,

223
00:18:01,261 --> 00:18:07,660
interference and constant starts and stops, and it's developed on the V-model,

224
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:13,586
with design and verification matched level by level, as the slides show.

225
00:18:13,986 --> 00:18:22,264
Two: sewing is an intermittent load; average loss sets the heat sink,

226
00:18:22,284 --> 00:18:26,594
peak current sets the junction jump,

227
00:18:26,614 --> 00:18:31,766
and lint on the shop floor eats the margin.

228
00:18:32,166 --> 00:18:38,201
Three: switching speed trades heat against noise and must sit in a window,

229
00:18:38,221 --> 00:18:42,621
and hot burn-in catches early failures at the factory.

